  package Foo; sub new { my $c = shift; print "new got ", scalar(@_), " arg(s)\n"; bless {}, $c }
  package main;
  sub ok2 { print scalar(@_), " [", ($_[1] // 'undef'), "]\n" }
  ok2 (new Foo, "named");
  ok2 (new Foo(), "named2");
perl 5.40.3:  new got 0 arg(s) / 2 [named] / new got 0 arg(s) / 2 [named2]
PCL (main ade297e7): new got 1 arg(s) / 1 [undef] / new got 0 arg(s) / 2 [named2]

perl's indirect object `new Foo` with no parenthesised argument list takes NO arguments when a comma follows (the comma ends it); PCL takes the rest of the list as new's arguments.  t/10_base.t:22 `ok (new Text::CSV, "Indirect object notation");` -- the row is the board's Text-CSV 10_base.t row 2 (re-caused to this task by s531b).  Layer: Pl/ (the indirect-object parse); docs/not-supported.md ns-indirect-object-scalar is about a SCALAR invocant, not this.

